From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:14:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 084/184] segacd: pause the drive after a FAILED read, not only
 a successful one

The user has described the same shape three times -- boots that get
slower run over run, a diagnostic that takes longer on its second
pass, a console that degrades from working desktop to white screen
over a session -- and it is not addresses, it is accumulation.

cd_fill pauses the drive at the end of a successful fetch. A failed
fetch returned early, and the last thing the drive was told was
'read', which makes it play forward until something says otherwise.
So every failure left the head travelling: the next read seeked back
from further out, took longer, failed more often, left it further
still. That is the feedback loop, and it is the head found at
01:80:00 while a read waited for 00:02:16.

docs/cdd-bios-notes.md records that Lunar's finisher -- CDC_STOP plus
ROM_PAUSEON -- runs on every completion AND every failure. Only the
completion half was ever transcribed. This is the other half.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 6f6e214..8466cac 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -2034,6 +2034,23 @@ collect:
 
 done:
     if (!got) {
+        /* Stop the drive running away.
+         *
+         * The last thing it was told was 'read', and a read command
+         * makes it play forward until something says otherwise. On
+         * success cd_fill pauses it; on failure nothing did, so every
+         * failed read left the head travelling, the next read seeked
+         * back from further out, took longer, failed more often --
+         * which is the deterioration reported across whole sessions
+         * and the head found at 01:80:00 while a read waited for
+         * 00:02:16.
+         *
+         * Lunar's finisher runs on every completion AND every failure;
+         * this driver had transcribed only the completion. Pausing
+         * here is the other half, and it is what stops a bad read from
+         * making the next one worse. */
+        cdd_ask_ready();
+        cd_streaming = 0;
         cd_errors++;
         /* Where, and is it the same where: the difference between a
          * marginal spot on the burn and a systemic fault is whether
